Answer
We now consider pleading from the defendant’s perspective. After the plaintiff files the complaint, the defendant may file a motion to dismiss. But the court may deny that motion, or the defendant may decline to file a motion to dismiss altogether. If so, the defendant must file an answer.

The answer is the initial pleading by the defendant. The answer is a pleading which responds to each of the allegations of the complaint and sets forth any additional allegations that the defendant wishes to include. In the answer, the defendant admits or denies the allegations in the complaint. A denial places that allegation’s factual claims in dispute, to be resolved through discovery, the presentation of evidence, and resolution of the factual question by judge or jury. An admission is a concession by the defendant of the truth of the allegation and removes the facts alleged from dispute. They are treated as true.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON ANSWERS, AFFIRMATIVE DEFENSES, AND RULE 8
We return now to Rule 8. We have already read Rule 8(a), which governs the complaint. Rule 8(b) governs the answer, and Rule 8(c) addresses affirmative defenses.

Rule 8(b) requires the answer to respond to each allegation of the complaint. The answer must admit or deny each allegation. If the defendant contends that an allegation is partly true, the defendant may admit the allegation in part and deny it in part, so long as the defendant specifies which part is admitted and which is denied. And if the defendant simply lacks knowledge of the allegation, Rule 8(b)(5) allows a defendant to plead that it does not have “information sufficient to form a belief about the truth of an allegation.”

As noted above, if a defendant fails to respond to an allegation in one of these ways, the allegation is treated as admitted if a responsive pleading was required. (If no responsive pleading was required, the allegation is not treated as admitted.) Once an allegation is admitted, it is considered established as true for purposes of the case.

Rule 8(c) requires a defendant to state any affirmative defenses in its answer. An affirmative defense states that even if the plaintiff’s claims are otherwise
