true, there are additional facts that—if proven—defeat the plaintiff’s entitlement to relief. For example, rather than deny that it breached a contract, the defendant could raise the affirmative defense that even if it did breach the contract, the suit was filed after the expiration of the relevant statute of limitations period. (A statute of limitations is a law that sets a time limit on when an action must be brought after the plaintiff discovers the injury.) If a defendant fails to raise an affirmative defense, a court may deem it forfeited. This is so even if the defense is valid.

There thus seems little room for error in pleading the answer, and the stakes are high. If the defendant admits an allegation it should have denied or fails to respond to an allegation, it is considered established, which may doom the defense. If the defendant fails to raise an affirmative defense, it is waived. As we shall see later in this chapter, however, the Rules create opportunities for both the plaintiff and defendant to amend their pleadings to correct mistakes or omissions.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 8
General Rules of Pleading

* * *

(b) Defenses; Admissions and Denials.

(1) In General. In responding to a pleading, a party must:

(A) state in short and plain terms its defenses to each claim asserted against it; and

(B) admit or deny the allegations asserted against it by an opposing party.

(2) Denials—Responding to the Substance. A denial must fairly respond to the substance of the allegation.

(3) General and Specific Denials. A party that intends in good faith to deny all the allegations of a pleading—including the jurisdictional grounds—may do so by a general denial. A party that does not intend to deny all the allegations must either specifically deny designated allegations or generally deny all except those specifically admitted.
